{"name":"Breaker","hex":"#e5eded","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"breaker","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Something that breaks (something else).","A machine for breaking rocks, or for breaking coal at the mines."]},{"part":"Interjection","definitions":["Used to open a conversation or call for a response on CB radio."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/breaker"}],"entries":[{"title":"Breaking wave","blocks":[{"text":"In fluid dynamics and nautical terminology, a break or breaker is a wave with enough energy to \"break\" at its peak, reaching a critical level at which linear energy transforms into wave turbulence energy with a distinct forward curve. At this point, simple physical models that describe wave dynamics often become invalid, particularly those that assume linear behaviour."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Large_breaking_wave.jpg/330px-Large_breaking_wave.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Breaking_wave"},{"title":"Circuit breaker","blocks":[{"text":"A circuit breaker is an electrical safety device designed to protect an electrical circuit from damage caused by current in excess of that which the equipment can safely carry (overcurrent). Its basic function is to interrupt current flow to protect equipment and to prevent fire. Unlike a fuse, which interrupts once and then must be replaced, a circuit breaker can be reset to resume normal operation."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fd/Jtecul.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Circuit_breaker"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Surfers_at_Mavericks.jpg/500px-Surfers_at_Mavericks.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/c